Embedded React: render scaled images and gradients wider than the row buffer.

Modified from Embedded React (Apache-2.0, Copyright 2026 Cory Lamming) at the
revision pinned in dependencies.lock.json, after the patches listed before this
one there. Changed files: engine/core/er_limits.h,
engine/rendering/image_scaler.c, engine/rendering/gradient.c and
engine/tests/rendering/CMakeLists.txt; new file
engine/tests/rendering/test_wide_rows.c.
Not proposed upstream: PS5 React maintains this change here.

A scaled or tinted image and a linear or radial gradient assemble each row in
a buffer of ERUI_MAX_IMG_ROW_PIXELS columns, and both clamped the box to it:
everything right of that many columns from the box's left edge was never
drawn. At 3840x2160 with a 2560-column buffer, a full-width cover image lost
its right third (more when it starts left of the screen). Both now walk the
visible span in chunks of the buffer's width.

A gradient row also started its fixed-point ramp from the first visible
column, so a column's colour moved by one step with the clip window. Rows now
start from box column 0, and a chunk or a damage-clipped repaint gives the
same pixels as a full one.

test_wide_rows renders boxes more than two buffers wide, starting left of the
framebuffer, in one pass and in clip strips narrower than the buffer. Every
column must be painted and the two renders must match.

